\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Logistic Regression Documentation\par
Start date 1/09/2026\par
1st Sept '26\par
-> Learned how logistic regression works. Summed down it is a method of machine learning where the algotiyhms works by rpedicting ctergorical outcomes whereas its counterpart linear regression works by predicting continous outcomes. The simplest case of logistic regression is where there is 2 out comes which is widley know as a boolean for example, True/False, Pass/Fail, 1/0 they all count as possible out comes in Logitsic Regression this form of outcome is identified as Binomial.\par
In the case that the out come is more than two, we call this multinomial for example, predicting the choice for mode of transport for an individual based of their fundings. This fits in the multinomial category as thier funds can lead to a reccomendation for any mode of transport as there are more than two such as Flight via Plane, Driving via Car, Cycling via Bike, Sailing, via boat etc.\par
2nd Sept '26\par
-> Learned the programming structure of Logistic regression especially when working with a dataset such as an array rather than a csv. When coding Logistic regression in python via scikit learn or any other meathods there is a solid set in stone structure that is recommended to follow and it goes as such.\par
-1 Data Splitting: Splitting the data into its required forms X and y where X is the Data in which we use to train the model and where y is the actual data we test the model on.\par
-2 Train test drive: This on is not so necesarry as its an optional thing to do many dont use it some do however it is recommended to use it this is where we split the data into 4 different arrays: X_Train (Data we train the ML on), X_Test(Data we Test the ML on), y_train(Answers the Ml learns from), y_Test (Answers we test the ML on). This is a bit difficult to udnerstand at first which is why i must say now this documentation is not for teaching rather taking you through my learning process in which minor things can be taken away from it to help you personally on your project journeys. Most of the data and learning came from W3Schools so Check out W3Schools on Machine Learning: {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://www.w3schools.com/python/python_ml_logistic_regression.asp }}{\fldrslt{https://www.w3schools.com/python/python_ml_logistic_regression.asp\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs22 . Continuing on, there are two more important thing we need to know abotu train test driving random_state, and test_size. Random state being a seed value used to control the pseudo random number generator that shuffles and divides your dataset. Setting it to a fixed integer ensures your train-test split is identical every single time you run your code. this can be any number but 42 is most used as it a reference to the book a hitch hikers guid to the galaxy (i probably got the title wrong i cant remember it well) where the answer to life in the book is 42. Whereas, test_size is the parameter that determines the percentage or absolute number of rows allocated to the test dataset. It specifies how much data to set aside for evaluating your model after it trains. for example 0.2 means 20% is allocated to the test set if this is left blank python automatically defaults it at 0.25 so 25%.\par
\par
-3 Scaling the data: This one is a bit hard to explain so ill leave a link for a documentation that explains it well: {{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://machinelearningmastery.com/standardscaler-and-minmaxscaler-transforms-in-python/ }}{\fldrslt{https://machinelearningmastery.com/standardscaler-and-minmaxscaler-transforms-in-python/\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs22\par
\par
-4 Predictions: This one is more simpler to explain as its literrally just to output the prediciton of your machine learning algorithm using print.\par
-5 Acurracy output (optional): This is more optional but is widley used as it is easier to maintain your algorithm allowing to see ways accuracy can be improved or reduced,(although im not too sure why you would want the accuracy to be reduced.) There is a specific way this is done \par

